\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Basic properties of incidence fields}
\date{2026-11-03}


\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring, and let #{V,E,s,h} and the
\vocabk{field modules}{fdip-0010},
\vocabk{field pullback}{fdip-0010} and
\vocabk{ring-valued incidence map}{fdip-0010} be as in that
definition. The pointwise operations make both modules commutative
unital #{R}-algebras, the indicator fields are a basis of each, every
field pullback preserves the operations, and #{d} is #{R}-linear.
Call two vertices equivalent when they are equal or a finite sequence
of edges has them as successive ends, ignoring orientation. The
equivalence classes are the undirected components; a vertex that lies
on no edge is a component of its own. Then #{df=0} if and only if
#{f} is constant on each undirected component.}

\proof{
\p{Every module and algebra identity follows by evaluation from the
corresponding identity in #{R}. For each #{v\in V}, let #{\delta_v(u)}
be #{1} if #{u=v} and #{0} otherwise. Evaluation at #{u} proves
##{f=\sum_{v\in V}f(v)\delta_v,}
and proves uniqueness of the coefficients. The same argument applies
to edge fields. On an empty carrier the sum is empty and there is one
field. Evaluation also proves every pullback law; for instance
#{a^*(fg)(e)=f(a(e))g(a(e))}. Finally
##{d(rf+g)(e)=r\bigl(f(h(e))-f(s(e))\bigr)+g(h(e))-g(s(e)),}
which proves linearity. These evaluations apply to the zero ring.}
\p{If #{df=0}, then #{f(h(e))=f(s(e))} for every edge, so #{f} takes
the same value at the successive ends of any finite sequence of edges
and is constant on each component. Conversely, the two ends of an edge
lie in one component, and the two ends of a loop are the same vertex,
so a field constant on each component has #{df=0}. The argument uses
no division.}
}
